================ what correlation concluded ====================
! A link went down and took hosts with it
   13:50:17 to 13:50:50  (33s)   rule link-down-isolated-hosts, confidence 80%
   affected: 10.99.1.11

   1  13:50:17.248  link.down  nrlab1
      An interface went down.
      |  which caused
   2  13:50:50.423  l2.neighbor_failed  10.99.1.11
      Hosts stopped answering at layer 2 immediately afterwards, which
      is the link failure showing up as apparent host failures.

   root cause: link.down on nrlab1 (confidence 80%)
   next:
      The hosts did not fail. Restore the link and they will return;
      investigating them individually wastes the outage.

! An address moved to a different machine
   13:50:20 to 13:50:20  (0s)   rule address-changed-hands, confidence 75%
   affected: 10.99.0.11

   1  13:50:20.378  l2.arp_binding_changed  10.99.0.11
      This address is now answered by hardware that was not previously
      bound to it, so the machine behind it is a different machine.

   root cause: l2.arp_binding_changed on 10.99.0.11 (confidence 75%)
   next:
      Normal after a DHCP lease expires. Worth a look if the address is
      a server, is statically assigned, or moved without a lease renewal
      near it.

!! The default gateway is being answered by a different machine
   13:50:21 to 13:50:50  (29s)   rule gateway-hijack, confidence 90%
   affected: 10.99.0.201, 10.99.1.11

   1  13:50:21.276  l2.arp_binding_changed  10.99.0.201
      The hardware address answering for the default gateway changed.
      Every host on this segment now sends its outbound traffic to a
      different machine.
      |  which caused
   2  13:50:27.338  l3.default_route_changed  default
      Routing followed the change, so traffic is now leaving through a
      path nobody chose.
      |  and at the same time
   3  13:50:50.423  l2.neighbor_failed  10.99.1.11
      Connectivity was lost while the gateway was being answered
      elsewhere.

   root cause: l2.arp_binding_changed on 10.99.0.201 (confidence 90%)
   next:
      Find which switch port the new hardware address is learned on
      before changing anything. A failover looks identical to an attack
      from here; the port tells them apart.

